For `File.WriteAllText`, `File.ReadAllText`, and `Directory.Exists`, the best approach is **`System.IO.Abstractions`** — a well-established NuGet package that wraps all `System.IO` statics behind injectable interfaces.

## Setup

**1. Install the packages:**

```bash
dotnet add <YourProject> package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add <YourTestProject> package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers
```

**2. Register in DI (Program.cs / Startup.cs):**

```csharp
builder.Services.AddSingleton<IFileSystem, FileSystem>();
```

**3. Inject `IFileSystem` into your classes:**

```csharp
public class ConfigManager(IFileSystem fileSystem)
{
    public string ReadConfig(string path)
        => fileSystem.File.ReadAllText(path);

    public void WriteConfig(string path, string content)
        => fileSystem.File.WriteAllText(path, content);

    public bool DirectoryExists(string path)
        => fileSystem.Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

**4. Test with `MockFileSystem`:**

```csharp
var mockFs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/config.json", new MockFileData("{\"key\": \"value\"}") }
});
// Pre-create the directory so Directory.Exists returns true
mockFs.AddDirectory("/data");

var manager = new ConfigManager(mockFs);

Assert.Equal("{\"key\": \"value\"}", manager.ReadConfig("/config.json"));
Assert.True(manager.DirectoryExists("/data"));

manager.WriteConfig("/output.json", "hello");
Assert.Equal("hello", mockFs.File.ReadAllText("/output.json"));
```

## Why this over a custom wrapper?

| Approach | Verdict |
|---|---|
| Custom `IFileService` | You'd have to wrap every method you use and maintain it yourself |
| **`System.IO.Abstractions`** | Battle-tested, covers the entire `System.IO` surface, includes `MockFileSystem` for tests |

This is the standard recommendation — no need to reinvent the wheel. Once the package is installed and registered, use the `migrate-static-to-wrapper` skill (or ask me) to bulk-replace your existing `File.*` / `Directory.*` call sites with the injected `IFileSystem`.
